  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Hibiscus Turret Bee is a solitary bee species belonging to the family Apidae, characterized by its robust, hairy body adapted for collecting pollen. Despite its common name, it is actually a bee rather than a plant, and it is found in New England where it plays an important role in pollinating native flora.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Robust, Fuzzy Body",
      "description": "Dense hair coverage on the body, particularly on the legs and abdomen, which helps with pollen collection",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Medium Size",
      "description": "Measures approximately 12-15 mm in length, making it similar in size to a bumblebee but more slender",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark Coloration",
      "description": "Predominantly dark brown to black body with lighter hair bands visible on the abdomen",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This solitary bee inhabits open woodlands, gardens, and meadows throughout New England where flowering plants and suitable nesting sites in soil or dead wood are available.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike honeybees, Hibiscus Turret Bees are solitary nesters that build individual turret-like mud structures to protect their eggs and larvae, giving them their distinctive common name.",
